"""Original trauma-informed service and cultural-responsiveness reasoning."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-057","Trauma Informed and Culturally Responsive Psychosocial Care","trauma-informed-and-culturally-responsive-psychosocial-care","Trauma Informed and\nCulturally Responsive\nPsychosocial Care",
 "Prepare a fictional psychosocial-service improvement that supports choice, cultural understanding and predictable communication while distinguishing supportive practice from trauma treatment and causal evidence.",[
m("Predictability choice and professional scope","Distinguish trauma-informed service conditions from trauma-focused treatment or diagnosis.",[
"A trauma-informed service considers how its routines, communication and use of power may affect people who have experienced adversity. A person should not need to disclose a trauma history to receive respectful, predictable assistance. Explain what an encounter is for, who is involved, what choices are available and how a person can ask to pause. Ask about communication and access needs rather than assume a particular reaction proves trauma. An introductory learner can examine these service conditions; determining a diagnosis, selecting treatment or conducting a clinical trauma interview requires an appropriately qualified role. [R29, S21, S1]",
"Choice should be meaningful within honestly described limits. A worker can explain available appointment arrangements, the purpose of a question and which information is necessary, without promising that every preference can be met. Respectful practice also recognises that an option may carry transport, cost, language or privacy barriers. Silence is not automatically understanding or agreement. A fictional support outline should separate what the person has actually requested, what the service can currently provide and what needs a qualified decision. Good intentions do not remove the effect of unnecessary pressure. [S21, S1]",
"The 2026 integrative review organises trauma-informed concepts across settings and distinguishes them from trauma-focused clinical interventions. Its searches end in December 2024, and rapid methods involve single-reviewer stages with checks on a subset of sources. It proposes a conceptual framework rather than estimates the effect of a treatment. Do not use a framework diagram, a staff training session or a revised reception script as proof of symptom improvement. A service change may be justified by rights and practical reasoning while its local effects still need an appropriate review. [R29, S21]",
"For this course, use a non-graphic fictional encounter to identify one avoidable source of uncertainty and a feasible adjustment. Describe the responsible role, what the person may influence and what remains outside the learner's authority. Do not ask classmates for personal experiences, conduct an exposure exercise, teach a therapy technique or interpret distress as evidence of a specific disorder. Where an actual concern exceeds the worker's role, authorised supervision and local clinical or safeguarding procedures matter. The worksheet assesses reasoned service design; it does not demonstrate competence to deliver independent trauma treatment. [S1, S21, R29]"],
"Fictional case: a reception desk asks an adult to repeat a private history in a shared waiting area before explaining the appointment. The learner proposes a private explanation and a choice about pacing without seeking the history or diagnosing the person.",
"Write a service-adjustment note with the avoidable uncertainty, an explanation to offer, a meaningful choice, an access constraint and the qualified decision boundary. Use no personal trauma account.",
"A useful note improves predictability and choice without requiring disclosure. It distinguishes a service condition from a diagnosis, a treatment intervention or proven recovery.",["R29","S21","S1"],[
("Respectful service does not require a disclosed trauma history.","A fictional reception process requires an adult to describe past trauma before receiving a clear appointment explanation. What should change?","Provide respectful information and appropriate choices without requiring that history.",["Require a more graphic account to establish eligibility.","Treat silence as proof that no support is needed.","Diagnose trauma from reluctance to speak publicly."],"Predictable respectful assistance need not depend on personal disclosure."),
("Trauma-informed conditions differ from trauma-focused treatment.","A learner improves privacy and explains an encounter's purpose. What has the learner done?","Addressed service conditions within scope rather than delivered a clinical trauma treatment.",["Completed a diagnostic examination.","Established that symptoms are cured.","Acquired authority to prescribe therapy."],"Organisational support and clinical intervention have different competencies."),
("An available option may still have access barriers.","A service offers an appointment that conflicts with an adult's work and transport. What should a support note consider?","Whether practical constraints limit the person's ability to use that option.",["That any offered slot proves access is equal.","That inability to attend is necessarily resistance.","That an unverified alternative can be promised as available."],"Meaningful choice depends on feasible access as well as a listed option."),
("Conceptual synthesis does not demonstrate treatment effects.","A team cites the 2026 trauma-informed framework review as proof its revised desk process cures distress. What is the correction?","The review supports conceptual appraisal; the local clinical-effect claim is unestablished.",["A diagram is equivalent to a treatment trial.","All settings necessarily achieve the same outcomes.","A recent publication removes the need to examine methods."],"The review synthesises concepts rather than testing this service change."),
("Course exercises should not become real trauma interviews.","A facilitator asks learners to interview classmates about trauma for marks. Which alternative fits this course?","Use the supplied fictional scenario and assess reasoning about service conditions.",["Reward the most detailed private history.","Record real disclosures in the slide notes.","Require an exposure exercise as proof of understanding."],"Introductory learning can be assessed without personal disclosure or clinical procedures."),
]),
m("Cultural understanding without stereotypes","Ask about meaning, identity and chosen supports while keeping evidence and individual preferences distinct.",[
"Cultural responsiveness begins with curiosity about the person's own meaning and priorities. Ask what language, explanations, relationships or community connections matter to them, using appropriate communication support. Do not assume an ethnic or religious identity determines beliefs, coping or desired care. The same family, faith setting or community can offer support to one person and create pressure for another. An interpreter or communication aid needs an appropriate role and privacy arrangements; a readily available relative is not automatically the person's preferred or safe interpreter. Keep the person involved in deciding how communication will work. [S1, S21, R34]",
"The July 2026 qualitative synthesis explores personal recovery among ethnic-minority people with severe mental illness in Western settings. It identifies varied accounts of family, faith, identity and agency. These accounts can suggest questions, but they do not establish what every Nigerian person wants, whether a particular relationship is safe or which intervention causes improvement. The review's UK-informed illness definitions, limited attention to differences within groups and broad age coverage constrain transfer. Avoid converting a theme into a rule that all families should participate or that spiritual support should replace qualified mental-health care. [R34, S1]",
"A local IJSDS provider survey in Ibadan raises relevant questions about beliefs, stigma, family support and service conditions. It uses a small purposive sample from one hospital, not representative service-user outcomes. Its analysis reporting contains inconsistencies, so this course does not teach its numerical findings or interpret them as causal evidence. The appropriate use is to keep local questions visible alongside stronger service guidance and wider syntheses. Nationality of a study does not remove the need to appraise methods, participant perspective and whether its findings fit the decision being considered. [R35, S21]",
"In a fictional conversation, distinguish a person's stated preference from the worker's interpretation and an unresolved question. Someone may value a community relationship while declining to share health information with that person. Supporting identity does not require accepting coercion, restricting access to care or assuming a group representative can consent for an adult. Identify the practical adjustment and the responsible qualified role if confidentiality, safeguarding or clinical questions arise. A culturally responsive plan should be revisitable: people may change their preferences as trust, circumstances or available options change. [S1, S21, R34]"],
"Fictional case: an adult values a faith community but does not want a community leader told about an appointment. A worker had assumed the leader should interpret. The learner separates valued connection from permission to share and considers an agreed communication option.",
"Draft a cultural-understanding note with the person's stated meaning, an assumption to avoid, a communication preference, a specific information boundary and an uncertainty requiring consultation.",
"A sound note asks rather than stereotypes. Valuing family, faith or identity is not blanket consent to involve others, and contextual studies cannot determine an individual's preferences.",["S1","S21","R34","R35"],[
("Group identity does not determine an individual's care preferences.","A worker assumes every member of a religious community wants the same support. What is a better approach?","Ask the person about their own priorities and desired connections.",["Apply one presumed preference to everyone.","Use identity as a diagnostic category.","Treat a community leader's view as every adult's consent."],"Cultural responsiveness recognises individual and within-group variation."),
("A valued relationship is distinct from permission to share.","A fictional adult values a faith community but declines information sharing with its leader. What distinction matters?","Connection can matter without authorising disclosure of health information.",["Valuing the community grants unlimited disclosure permission.","The worker may override preferences for cultural consistency.","Any spiritual support replaces clinical care."],"Support preferences and specific information permissions are separate."),
("An available relative is not automatically an appropriate interpreter.","A relative offers to interpret a private appointment discussion. What should be considered?","The person's preference, privacy needs and appropriate communication-support arrangements.",["Availability alone establishes suitability.","Family status proves the person agreed.","Interpretation removes the need to explain roles."],"Communication support must be suitable and agreed within the setting."),
("Local evidence still requires critical appraisal.","A small single-hospital provider survey is described as proof of what all Nigerian service users experience. What is missing?","Attention to sampling, whose perspective was measured and limits of generalisation.",["Only a local author affiliation.","A guarantee that provider views equal patient outcomes.","Permission to ignore inconsistent analysis reporting."],"A relevant setting does not make a limited design representative or causal."),
("Qualitative themes are prompts rather than universal rules.","A review describes family support and family pressure in recovery accounts. How should a learner use that finding?","Ask about the person's actual relationships and preferences without presuming benefit.",["Require family involvement for every adult.","Conclude no family can ever help.","Treat a theme as an individual outcome prediction."],"Varied accounts inform contextual questions rather than fixed instructions."),
]),
m("Organisational response and accountable learning","Propose a feasible service improvement with lived-experience input and bounded review.",[
"A supportive encounter depends partly on organisational conditions: workloads, privacy, communication access, staff preparation and accepted decision roles. A policy promising choice is insufficient if every process pressures people to disclose or accept one option. The 2025 WHO policy introduction connects service reform with workforce, governance and social conditions. For a fictional service review, identify which rule or resource creates a barrier and who can change it. Avoid framing an organisational failure as a personal lack of resilience or asking an individual worker to guarantee outcomes beyond their authority. [S22, S21, S1]",
"Lived-experience involvement should influence a stated decision through safe, accessible and appropriately supported arrangements. It need not require people to recount traumatic experiences or represent every member of a group. Explain what feedback can change, the limits of confidentiality and how a response will be provided. A person should be able to disagree without losing assistance. This course uses fictional feedback only; actual consultation requires appropriate governance and safeguarding. Do not interpret a low response rate as universal approval or remove a critical account because it complicates the proposed service story. [S21, S22, S1]",
"Evidence appraisal should remain visible in a recommendation. The trauma-informed review offers conceptual organisation, the cultural-recovery review provides contextual qualitative accounts and the Ibadan study raises local provider questions with substantial limitations. None alone proves that a new reception procedure reduces symptoms. State the specific change being tested, who can use it, a relevant observation and the limits of inference. A clearer appointment explanation is an output; whether intended people understand and can exercise choices is a separate review question. Clinical recovery would require different expertise and evidence. [R29, R34, R35, S21]",
"Write a proportionate action note with an authorised owner, resource dependency, accessible feedback route and review point. If a barrier cannot yet be resolved, record it and seek the responsible decision rather than promise an unavailable service. Staff support and supervision should address uncertainty, boundaries and workload without turning a short reflective task into treatment or competency certification. The goal is an accountable improvement in service conditions that can be reviewed. Independent clinical, lived-experience, legal and accessibility appraisal remains necessary before this draft becomes part of an approved learner curriculum. [S22, S21, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a service rewrites its welcome leaflet but still has no private space or language support. A report calls the leaflet proof of recovery. The learner identifies the unresolved conditions and proposes a bounded review of understanding and choice.",
"Prepare an improvement brief with the barrier, change, owner, resource dependency, lived-experience input, review question and unsupported claim to remove. Distinguish an output from a service-access or clinical outcome.",
"A defensible brief connects organisational conditions to a feasible action and honest review. It includes meaningful input and supervision without claiming that a leaflet, framework or course badge proves clinical recovery.",["S22","S21","S1","R29","R34","R35"],[
("Organisational barriers require appropriate authority and resources.","A fictional policy promises private discussion but no private space exists. What should a recommendation identify?","The resource gap and the role authorised to resolve or manage it.",["Only the worker's attitude.","A guarantee that written policy creates space.","A demand that service users become more resilient."],"Service conditions depend on organisational decisions as well as communication."),
("Lived-experience input should influence a defined decision.","A service seeks feedback but cannot explain what may change. What needs clarification?","The decision scope, access arrangements and accountable response to input.",["A requirement to share a trauma narrative.","A claim that one contributor represents everyone.","A rule that disagreement affects eligibility for support."],"Meaningful involvement needs influence and appropriate conditions."),
("Producing information differs from usable understanding.","A welcome leaflet is rewritten. Which separate review question fits the change?","Whether intended people understand the process and can exercise the explained choices.",["Whether the print run proves clinical recovery.","Whether producing text removes every service barrier.","Whether the leaflet is equivalent to a treatment trial."],"An output does not establish its intended access or understanding outcome."),
("Evidence types constrain claims about an improvement.","A recommendation combines a conceptual review, qualitative accounts and a limited provider survey. What is justified?","A reasoned proposal with evidence limits and a need to review local effects.",["A proven causal symptom reduction in every setting.","An individual diagnosis from the combined citations.","Permission to omit lived-experience feedback."],"Several sources can inform judgement without proving a local intervention effect."),
("An unresolved dependency should remain visible.","A fictional service cannot yet provide an agreed communication option. What should the plan state?","The limitation, responsible decision route and feasible follow-up rather than an availability promise.",["That the option is guaranteed because it is desirable.","That the person caused the service gap.","That all future reviews can be cancelled."],"An honest plan identifies conditions requiring action and review."),
])])
